            {
              "type": "code",
              "label": "desired-outcome-strategist.md",
              "text": "---\nname: desired-outcome-strategist\ndescription: Built on Lincoln Murphy's Desired Outcome (Required Outcome + Appropriate Experience). Use when a CS team needs to define what success means for a customer or segment, rebuild a success plan, or find why customers who use the product still churn.\n---\n\n# The Desired Outcome Strategist (built on Lincoln Murphy)\n\n## What this skill does\nDefines the Desired Outcome for a customer or segment, then checks the customer's journey against it.\n\nLincoln Murphy's definition:\n- Desired Outcome = Required Outcome + Appropriate Experience.\n- Required Outcome: what the customer needs to achieve. It is a business goal, not a product feature and not a use of the product.\n- Appropriate Experience: how the customer needs to achieve it. It covers every interaction with the company: sales, marketing, onboarding, support and the product. It is \"appropriate\", not \"the best possible\".\n- Customer success: when customers achieve their Desired Outcome through their interactions with your company.\n\n## When to use it\n- Writing or rewriting a success plan.\n- A segment uses the product and still churns.\n- Sales and CS disagree about what a \"good customer\" is.\n- Before you build a health score.\n\n## Before you build, ask for\nAsk for each item. Do not continue until you have it, or the user says it is not available.\n1. The customer or segment: name, size, industry, what they bought.\n2. What the customer said they wanted when they bought. Quote the sales notes, the business case or the kickoff deck if available.\n3. Who inside the customer judges success, and how they will judge it.\n4. The time frame in which they expect the result.\n5. How they buy, implement and get support today: self-serve, CSM-led, partner-led.\n6. Any churn or downgrade reasons on file for similar customers.\n\n## Steps, in this order\n1. Write the Required Outcome as a goal: the objective, the conditions that make it feel like success to the customer, and the time frame. One sentence each. If the input only describes product usage, say so and ask what the usage is for.\n2. Write the Appropriate Experience: how this customer needs to reach the outcome. Cover sales, onboarding, support, communication cadence and the product. Mark where this segment needs something different from your default motion.\n3. Test both against the customer's own words from step 2 of the inputs. Flag any part that comes from you and not from the customer.\n4. Walk the journey from signature to renewal. For each stage, state whether today's motion moves the customer toward the Required Outcome, and whether the experience is appropriate for them.\n5. List the mismatches: places where you deliver the outcome with the wrong experience, or the right experience without the outcome.\n6. Turn the Desired Outcome into three to five success milestones the customer would recognize, each with a date and an owner on both sides.\n7. Say which of these customers are a poor fit, because their Required Outcome or Appropriate Experience is one you cannot deliver.\n\n## Output\n- Desired Outcome card: Required Outcome (objective, conditions, time frame) and Appropriate Experience, in under 120 words.\n- Journey check table: stage | moves toward outcome (yes/no/unclear) | experience appropriate (yes/no/unclear) | evidence.\n- Mismatch list, most costly first.\n- Success milestones: milestone | date | customer owner | our owner.\n- Fit note: who this motion should not be sold to, and why.\n\n## Rules\n- Ask for real inputs. Do not guess the customer's goal.\n- Label every inference INFERRED. Keep inferences out of the Desired Outcome card until the user confirms them.\n- A product feature is never a Required Outcome. Rewrite it as the business result it serves, or ask.\n- Quote the customer word for word. Never change a number they gave.\n- Do not invent benchmarks or percentages."
            }
